  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "O.K.Francis vs State-Food Inspector on 12 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Tuesday, The 12Th Day Of January 2016/22Nd Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 3826 of 2007 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 264/2004 of Addl. Sessions Court (Adhoc)-Ii, Kalpetta Cc 309/2002 of J.M.F.C.,Sulthanbathery Revision Petitioners/Appellants/Accused: ----------------------------------------------------------------- 1. O.K.Francis, S/O.Kurian, Ozhangal Veedu, Nadavayal Post, Wayanad District. 2. P.A.Devassia, S/O.Augusthy, Puthuparambil House, Nadavayal Post, Wayanad District. By Adv. Sri.Grashious Kuriakose Respondent/Complainant: ----------------------------------------------- State-Food Inspector, Mananthavady Circle, Rep. By The Public Prosecutor High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. By Public Prosecutor, Shri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 12-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - Crl.R.P. No.3826 of 2007 O.K.Francis vs State-Food Inspector on 12 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/168204283/ 1 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 12th day of January 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "Heard. 3. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 13.11.2000 at 8.15 a.m., PW1, the Food Inspector, purchased 20 ice sticks from the shop of the second accused. First accused was the vendor. After complying with the legal formalities, samples were taken. Thereafter, one of the samples was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst, after complying with the legal formalities. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P15 report stating that the sample contained artificial sweetner, like saccharine and therefore, the same was adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P19 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory would show that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for ice scandy and therefore, it was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts. P1 to P24 were marked for the prosecution. No evidence was adduced on the side of the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P19 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P19 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and O.K.Francis vs State-Food Inspector on 12 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/168204283/ 2",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P19 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P19 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and O.K.Francis vs State-Food Inspector on 12 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/168204283/ 2"
  },
